---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import Chevron from '../components/Chevron.astro';
import { audit } from '../data/palette.mjs';
import { REPO, REPO_LABEL } from '../data/site.mjs';
import { principles, requirements, PREVIEW_PAIRS } from '../data/rubric.mjs';

/**
 * Three beats: before a system exists, the moment it starts to constrain, and
 * after. Written from that shape rather than assembled from the method page and
 * the colophon, which is what the previous version was.
 *
 * The six brief questions and the six review criteria are ledgers here, not
 * headed sections with a paragraph each. They were the argument when the page
 * was a list of four things I build. They are reference material inside phase
 * two now, and explaining each one twice was the fluff.
 */
const rows = audit();
const passing = rows.filter((r) => r.pass).length;
const preview = PREVIEW_PAIRS.map(([fg, bg]) => rows.find((r) => r.fg === fg && r.bg === bg)).filter(
  Boolean,
);

const proof = [
  ['16', 'Static pages'],
  ['0', 'Client-side scripts on content pages'],
  [`${passing} / ${rows.length}`, 'Colour pairs passing'],
  ['10', 'Old essay URLs preserved'],
];

/** What a brief has to answer. Writable before anything exists. */
const brief = [
  ['Intent', 'Who is trying to do what, and which decision should get easier?'],
  ['Required behavior', 'What has to hold across every state, transition, permission and failure path?'],
  ['System constraints', 'Which tokens, components and patterns must the work resolve to?'],
  ['Prohibited actions', 'What may the agent not do without a person?'],
  ['Open questions', 'What should the work help us resolve?'],
  ['Evaluation criteria', 'What would make this wrong even if it looked finished?'],
];

/** What I read a generated screen against. */
const lenses = [
  ['Intent', 'Does it solve the problem we agreed to solve?'],
  ['Behavior', 'Do the states, transitions, permissions and failure paths make sense?'],
  ['System', 'Does it strengthen the product’s patterns, or erode them?'],
  ['Content', 'Does it explain what happened and what to do next?'],
  ['Accessibility', 'Can people perceive, understand and operate it in real conditions?'],
  ['Implementation', 'Is the behavior credible in the actual product?'],
];

/** The three places an agent would otherwise decide by default. */
const limits = [
  ['Invent a system value', 'If no approved value exists, name the gap. Do not fill it.'],
  ['Resolve product ambiguity', 'An assumption made at speed is still an assumption, and it arrives looking finished.'],
  ['Treat valid as approved', 'Passing every check is not the same as being worth building.'],
];
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I define the conditions in which AI produces design work"
  metaTitle="I design with AI, and this site is the evidence | Tyler Young"
  description="Before a design system exists, you cannot specify it — you build real output until the patterns show. After it exists, it constrains what gets built. The work reverses at that point, and this site is the worked example."
  eyebrow="AI-assisted product design"
  current="building"
  meta={[
    ['Practice', 'AI-assisted product design'],
    ['My role', 'Frame · constrain · evaluate · decide'],
    ['This site', 'Astro · Tailwind · static · no client JavaScript'],
    ['Built', 'From nothing, in four days'],
  ]}
  links={[
    { label: 'The design system', href: '/design-system' },
    { label: REPO_LABEL, href: REPO },
  ]}
>
  <p class="lede">
    An agent can build a polished interface in minutes. It cannot tell you
    whether that interface is the right one.
  </p>
  <p>
    So my work moved to the conditions the agent produces inside. Those
    conditions change once, sharply, at the point a design system becomes real
    enough to constrain what gets built. Everything about how I work reverses
    there.
  </p>

  <h2>Before the system exists</h2>
  <p>
    A brief can be written before anything exists. A design system cannot.
  </p>
  <p>
    A system is a set of families — of surface, ink, type, space, mark,
    sentence — and a family is invisible in a single instance. You need enough
    real output to compare before there is anything to standardise.
  </p>
  <p>
    So I write the brief, then move to running software fast and look for what
    repeats. Speed is not the point. Legibility is.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      brief.map(([label, question]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{question}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    This site is the worked example. Four days, from nothing. Every system
    decision in it arrived as a defect first:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      one alternating row treatment was five implementations across four files,
      invisible until two were seen disagreeing
    </li>
    <li>one heading construction was a tic repeated twenty times, invisible until counted</li>
    <li>pull-quotes on every page were restating the paragraph above them</li>
    <li>one banded colour took six rendered rounds, because the value was only judgeable against real content</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Each began as one wrong thing on one page and ended as a change to the
    system. That is the move: fix the family, not the instance.
  </p>
  <p>
    The standard, though, was written first — before any of it existed. That is
    the half of the work that does not need the output.
  </p>

  <ul class="principles">
    {
      principles.map((p) => (
        <li>
          <p class="principle">{p.principle}</p>
          <p class="principle-detail">{p.detail}</p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2>The tipping point</h2>
  <p>
    The system becomes real when it can answer a question before the work
    starts. Until then it is a record of what was built. After it, it decides
    what may be.
  </p>
  <p>The direction of the work reverses at that line.</p>
  <p>
    Before: output produces the system. I am reading generated work to find
    families.
  </p>
  <p>
    After: the system produces the output. I am setting constraints, and the
    agent reports a gap instead of inventing a local answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Miss the crossing and you keep hand-correcting instances of a rule you
    already know — which is the expensive failure, because AI makes producing
    those instances almost free.
  </p>

  <h2>After the system exists</h2>
  <p>
    Every colour, type style, spacing value, radius, component and accessibility
    rule resolves to the system. Where a value does not exist, the agent names
    the gap. The system stops being a document someone checks against and
    becomes the environment the interface is generated inside.
  </p>
  <p>
    At Atheer I rebuilt an ageing system into that kind of pipeline.
    Explorations came back as working components, engineers received behaviour
    as code, and brand and accessibility rules applied before review instead of
    after it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/ops-manager">The product and the system it was built from</a> are both
    running.
  </p>
  <p>
    Generating three directions is cheap now; choosing between them is not. I
    build them, deploy each to its own URL, and read every one against six
    questions.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      lenses.map(([label, q]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{q}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    A screen can be clean, consistent and still wrong. Running software also
    exposes what a specification hides — two people using one word for two
    behaviours, an agent satisfying a sentence and missing its reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a class="link" href="/review-loop">A stakeholder review run this way</a> is on the site: two
    running builds, forty-eight notes, and the three where the written request
    and the right decision were not the same.
  </p>

  <h2>What holds on both sides</h2>
  <p>
    Three decisions stay mine regardless of how much system exists, because they
    are the places an agent would otherwise decide by default.
  </p>

  <ol class="loop">
    {
      limits.map(([label, note]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="loop-actor">{label}</span>
          <span class="loop-what">{note}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>

  <p>
    In <a class="link" href="/alucify">Alucify</a> I designed the surfaces that make those
    distinctions visible in a product. Here they are enforced by the build.
  </p>

  <h2 id="this-site">What gets checked, and by whom</h2>
  <p>
    A perimeter is only real if something enforces it. Every requirement on this
    site names its verifier, and the page says which are machine-checked and
    which a person reads, because those are not the same promise.
  </p>

  <ul class="ledger">
    {
      requirements.map((r) => (
        <li class:list={['gate', { human: r.mode === 'HUMAN REVIEW' }]}>
          <p class="gate-req">{r.req}</p>
          <p class="gate-meta">
            <span class="gate-mode">{r.mode}</span>
            <span class="gate-how">{r.how.replace('{pairs}', String(rows.length))}</span>
          </p>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    Colour is the clearest case. Every pairing is checked at the smallest size
    it appears, and two failed during this build. Both times the token changed
    rather than the component — the family, not the instance.
  </p>

  <p class="result-line">{passing} of {rows.length} documented colour pairs pass.</p>

  <table class="ratios">
    <caption class="sr-only">
      Four representative colour pairs, computed at build time from src/data/palette.mjs
    </caption>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th scope="col">Pair</th>
        <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
        <th scope="col">Needs</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        preview.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <td>
              <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                Aa
              </span>
              {r.fg} on {r.bg}
            </td>
            <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
            <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>
\n  <details class="all-pairs">
    <summary><Chevron weight="bold" />View all {rows.length} colour-pair results</summary>
    <div class="scroller">
      <table class="ratios">
        <caption class="label">
          Computed at build time from <code>src/data/palette.mjs</code> — {passing} of{' '}
          {rows.length} pairs passing
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Pair</th>
            <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
            <th scope="col">Needs</th>
            <th scope="col">Used for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>
                  <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                    Aa
                  </span>
                  {r.fg} on {r.bg}
                </td>
                <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
                <td class="use">{r.use}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </details>

  <ul class="cells four">
    {
      proof.map(([figure, label]) => (
        <li>
          <span class="cell-figure">{figure}</span>
          <span class="cell-label">{label}</span>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <p>
    The code is public, and reading it is the one check I cannot run on my own
    behalf.
  </p>

  <h2>What changed is the material</h2>
  <p>
    I am not a frontend engineer and I do not think designers need to become
    one. What changed is where the leverage sits. When each screen was expensive
    to make, the work was making them. When working software is cheap, the work
    is the environment producing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tools are new. The standard is not: useful, coherent, accessible,
    explainable, and right for the people it serves.
  </p>
</DocumentLayout>
